    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter June 20th with a clear mandate: prioritize speed and high-ceiling leadoff spots to combat the league's scarcity in stolen bases. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (68.27) stands as today\u2019s anchor, benefiting immensely from a soft matchup against Corbin (4.62 xERA) and the prime #1 lineup slot, which maximizes his opportunity to swipe bags. Similarly, **Trea Turner** (66.41) is a mandatory start; while he faces a tough arm in Peralta, the underlying metrics favor his elite sprint speed and a high-probability steal environment created by the pitcher's slow delivery. We are also leaning heavily on **Yordan Alvarez** (66.32), whose raw power remains locked in at an elite 0.450 xwOBA, proving that his immense potential to drive in runs outweighs the slight handicap of a L/L platoon disadvantage against Cantillo.\n\nThe decision-making process was particularly difficult regarding the bottom of the order, where **Casey Schmitt** (44.51) barely survived the benching threshold. We opted to keep him in the lineup to maintain our category exposure, though we are monitoring his performance closely against Meyer. The most significant benching decision involved **Gary Sanchez** (39.85), who agonizingly missed the 40.0 \"Below Floor\" cutoff. While **Gary Sanchez** possessed some positive platoon momentum, his projected struggle against Sale (3.01 xERA) effectively neutralized any offensive utility today. As we look at our depth, names like **Carson Benge** (38.60) were also discarded; despite their potential, the Zebras prioritize high-efficiency, high-floor matchups to preserve game caps, meaning fringe starters like **Carson Benge** remain on standby should our starters falter early in their respective series.",
